            Running costs add a few hundred dollars a year for a gas machine. Fuel, oil, filters, plugs, blades and the occasional belt for a residential rider typically run around $150 to $300 per season depending on lawn size and how much of the service you do yourself. Electric riders cut that sharply, since they have no oil, filters, plugs or fuel, which is a real part of their long-term value case.
          </p>

          <p>
            Over ten years, the purchase price and the repair bill usually matter more than routine running costs. A cheap machine that needs a transmission or an engine in year five can cost more in total than a mid-range machine that simply keeps going. That is why our <a href="/best-riding-lawn-mower/">riding lawn mower rankings</a> weight build quality, engine reputation and deck construction so heavily.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before pricing machines, settle what size and type you need. Our guide to <a href="/what-size-riding-mower-do-i-need/">what size riding mower you need</a> walks through deck width by acreage and the gate and slope checks, and buying the right size is the single biggest lever on value. An oversized machine costs more up front, burns more fuel and may not fit where it needs to go.
          </p>

