            2. Price by Machine Class
          </h2>

          <p>
            Rear-engine riders are the smallest and simplest class, with decks typically around 30 inches. They are aimed at lots too small for a full lawn tractor or at owners who need to ride for mobility reasons, and they generally cost about the same as an entry lawn tractor despite their smaller size. They make sense where storage space and gate width rule out anything larger.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lawn tractors are the mainstream residential choice: front engine, steering wheel, 42 to 54-inch decks. Entry models with manual or basic hydrostatic transmissions and stamped decks sit at the bottom of the price range. Mid-range models add better hydrostatic transmissions, twin-cylinder engines, cast-iron front axles and sometimes fabricated decks. The jump from entry to mid-range is where durability improves most per dollar.
          </p>

          <p>
            Garden tractors are heavier, built with stronger frames, larger rear tires, more robust transmissions and often the ability to run ground-engaging attachments such as tillers. They typically start around $4,500 to $5,000 and climb well beyond, and they make sense for large properties, hilly ground or owners who will genuinely use them for more than mowing. For lighter-duty buyers they are more machine than needed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Residential zero-turns span a wide range. Entry models with stamped decks and light-duty transaxles start around the price of a mid-range tractor, while machines with fabricated decks, commercial-grade transmissions and larger engines run into the $5,000 to $8,000 range. Electric riders, both tractors and zero-turns, carry a premium over comparable gas machines, partly offset by lower running costs.
          </p>

