        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Most residential riding mowers cost between about $1,500 and $5,000 to buy and a few hundred dollars a year to run, but the number that matters is the ten-year total. Entry machines often need replacing or major repairs within five or six seasons, which erases their head start over a mid-range tractor that simply keeps going.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Spend on the transmission, the deck and the engine, save on size and comfort extras, and do routine maintenance yourself. That combination buys a machine you replace once a decade or less, which is the cheapest way to own a riding mower.
          </p>
